    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1-a8">

                <pb facs="0024" n="14" xml:id="pg14"/>

                <head>Appendix H to HD–85</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >commander-in-chief<lb/>the allied armies<lb/>general staff</hi>
                        <lb/> No. 5249</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>G. H. Q.,</placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1919-11-03">November 3,
                        1919</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">From: Marshal Foch.</p>
                <p rendition="#flush">To: The President of the Council, the President of the Peace
                    Conference (Secretariat General).</p>
                <p>By letter No. 146, under date of October 21, 1919,<anchor n="18" xml:id="d1-f1"/>
                    General Nollet, President of the Military Commission of Control in Germany,
                    requested you to be kind enough to have the Supreme Council decide whether the
                    pay of Officers and troops in the Commissions of Control should be included in
                    the upkeep expenses of the said Commissions, and later be charged to
                    Germany.</p>
                <p>No decision has been taken on this subject.</p>
                <p>It follows that General Nollet experiences great difficulty in recruiting the
                    civil personnel necessary,—interpreters and engineers—this personnel at the
                    present time receiving no pay, but only having the right to allowances allotted
                    to the Members of Commissions of Control.</p>
                <p>This state of affairs is prejudicial to the good operation of the Commissions of
                    Control.</p>
                <p>Consequently, I have the honor to ask you to be kind enough to advise the Supreme
                    Council of this question as soon as possible.</p>
                <closer>P. O. The Major General<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Weygand</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
